//? Title: AlexNet: Grouped Filters, Channel Normalisation and Dropout
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A convolutional classifier needs enough learned filters to describe images without making
every layer prohibitively expensive. AlexNet combines grouped filters, rectified activations,
overlapping pooling and dropout. We isolate its channel connectivity and normalisation, verify
full reference geometry, then expose every tensor Adam update in a compact CIFAR-10 fit.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.2_classic_architectures/alexnet.cpp`.

> The method

The five convolution widths are 96, 256, 384, 384 and 256. Layers 2, 4 and 5 use two
groups: an output group reads only its contiguous half of the preceding channels. For a
k×k kernel, G groups reduce kernel weights and MACs per output location from k² Cin Cout
to k² Cin Cout/G; biases add Cout. This is a connectivity restriction, not simply a faster
evaluation of an unrestricted dense kernel. The report compares separate-group convolution
with the core grouped kernel, including every input and parameter derivative.

Local response normalisation across channels is

$$
y_c=x_c\left(b+\alpha\sum_{j=\max(0,c-r)}^{\min(C-1,c+r)}x_j^2\right)^{-\beta}.
$$
\alttext(Each activation is divided by a power of a positive offset plus neighbouring channel energy.)

The architecture uses r=2, b=2, alpha=.0001 and beta=.75: alpha multiplies a sum, with no
window-width divisor. This follows the paper's constants; the BVLC deployment supplies
227-pixel geometry, not identical LRN defaults. A radius-one toy x=(1,2,3), b=2, alpha=.1
gives denominators (2.5,3.4,3.3), including clipped edge windows. Tensor matmul against a
constant channel band implements the sum; its O(C²) band storage and O(numel(x)C) work are
an explicit CPU adaptation, rather than a fused linear-time sliding-window kernel.

The VALID 11×11 stride-four stem maps 227 to 55. Three overlapping VALID 3×3 stride-two
pools give 27,13,6. SAME convolutions between pools preserve size. Two 4096-unit ReLU dense
layers precede 1000 logits; the full model has 60965224 learned scalars. In training, inverted
dropout retains half the hidden units and divides survivors by .5; inference uses every unit.
He/Glorot initializers and the compact demonstration's Adam differ from the original recipe.

The dataset experiment retains all five convolutions, three pools, groups, LRN and dropout,
but uses 67-pixel bilinear resizing, quarter convolution widths and 256-unit dense layers.
Its 128 fitting, 64 validation and 64 official-test images are a bounded teaching experiment.
It runs 20 epochs, independent padded crops/flips, Adam(.001), and kernel L2=.0001/2.
Validation selects the checkpoint before a test pass. Cached CIFAR-10 is required.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/alexnet_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/alexnet_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/alexnet_concept.tikz > build/alexnet_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/alexnet_concept.svg build/alexnet_concept.dvi > build/alexnet_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
